L wrote:
> Personally I like the patch but feel that the mechanic itself doesn't
> make sense. Y'see, scroll magic is activated with a verbal component,
> and the act of hitting with them constitutes a gestural ("somatic")
> component. Also, I think it makes scroll identification (destroy armor,
> fire etc.) undeservedly easier.

Again, it's magic, it doesn't always make sense the way you'd expect.
Not all scroll types have effects then thrown, and the effects of
throwing the scroll are usually weaker than reading it.  And bring in
verbal and gestural components implies tying the game to D&D more than
is perhaps necessary.

Note that Shiren the Wanderer (the fairly awesome Japanese roguelike
for the Super Famicom) also has effects for thrown scrolls, indeed
scrolls of genocide in that game are properly used by throwing them, so
this isn't even the first time the idea's been seen.


> (Also! The "roll" for scroll magic activation appears to be a Dex
> check. Why dexterity? Must you hit with just the right part of the
> scroll or something? And surely you should factor in your increase
> accuracy rings as well?)

This is a more serious problem.  I think the chances of a scroll
hitting should be an ordinary thrown item check, if it's not then
that's a problem.

Further question: does the patch take into account the possibility of
wielding a scroll?

